OUTPUT TEXT: "a-fatbin** (fatbinary embedded in the Rust binary). Embedded fatbin avoids runtime NVRTC latency and avoids the CUDA 13.1 nvcc codegen bug for MMQ. |\n| Kernel launch | **`cudarc`** `launch_kernel` (driver API) or `cudarc` cuBLASLt wrapper (for dense GEMM fallback) | launch via `cudarc` driver API |\n| Server | **`axum` + `tokio`** (OpenAI-compatible HTTP, SSE streaming) |\n| Model format | **GGUF** (primary), **Safetensors** (secondary, for MoE research) |\n\n### Why Rust + embedded fatbin over C++/Python?\n\n| Criterion | Rust + fatbin | C++/Python (vLLM/SGLang) | llama.cpp (C++) |\n||---|---|---|\n| **Zero-copy KV / spill mgr** | Single address space, no GC, `Arc<Mutex<>>` for shared state, `unsafe` blocks scoped to kernel launches | Python GIL + GC pauses; C++ vLLM still pays Python dispatch tax | C++ is fine, but per-op dispatch + re-copy hot experts every token |\n| memory safety | Rust ownership + borrow checker prevents use-after-free in KV cache / spill mgr | Manual memory management (C++) or GC (Python) | Manual memory management |\n| Build reproducibility | `cargo` + `build.rs` → deterministic fatbin | Python wheels + pip deps + CUDA toolkit version hell | CMake + manual CUDA toolkit versioning |\n| **MoE expert offload** | Rust can manage host RAM + NVMe + VRAM as a single coherent pool | Python engines OOM on >24 GB MoE | llama.cpp offloads but re-copies hot experts every token |\n\n### Why embedded fatbin over NVRTC?\n\n| Criterion | Embedded fatbin | NVRTC |\n||---|---|\n| **Startup latency** | Zero (fatbin already compiled) | 2–5 s per kernel (NVRTC compile time) |\n| **CUDA 13.1 nvcc bug** | Avoided (AOT compile with CUDA 12.8) | Exposed (NVRTC uses CUDA 13.1 runtime) |\n| graphs | Fatbin kernels can be captured in CUDA"
